      "technique_id": "multiphoton_microscopy",
      "property_id": "internal_3d_structure",
      "technique": "Multiphoton microscopy",
      "why": "Multiphoton microscopy allows for deeper imaging than standard confocal microscopy (up to several hundred micrometers) in fluorescent or collagen-rich samples, though it cannot image through opaque materials.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "tunable infrared multiphoton laser",
        "non-descanned detectors",
        "water-dipping objective"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique_id": "micro_computed_tomography",
      "technique": "X-ray micro-computed tomography (micro-CT)",
      "why_useful": "Micro-CT is the standard non-destructive method for visualizing the full internal 3D structure and quantifying porosity in opaque or thick porous specimens."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "No listed instrument can perform non-destructive 3D internal imaging of opaque porous specimens; X-ray micro-computed tomography (micro-CT) is the standard technique for this purpose. As a partial alternative, confocal or multiphoton microscopy can provide 3D structural data, but these are limited to shallow depths and require the sample to be transparent or fluorescently labeled."
}

